{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1532728-soul-mechanics-and-religious-mages","id":1532728,"title":"Soul Mechanics and Religious Mages","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-03-16T15:16:54-07:00","id":1532728,"title":"Soul Mechanics and Religious Mages","content":"How do you handle the disconnect between the theologies of player characters and the manner in which the game's mechanics treat souls? In particular, the ontology of the soul that the setting presents is difficult to reconcile with Mormon and Jain models of the universe. Have any issues regarding the the topic come up in your games? It seems like the easiest option is to just suppose that what the game treats as a soul is not necessarily a soul, but I am curious to see how others might have approached the problem in their games.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28352-verlan","name":"Verlan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"118"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-03-16T15:19:34-07:00","id":1532729,"title":"","content":"No, I'm not sure what the issue is.<br /> <br /> The game is not the beliefs of Mormons or Jains, it is a game setting. I don't expect things to work in it the way anyone believes it works in reality.<br /> <br /> Also, what is the contradiction you see? Nobody knows what happens to souls when a person dies.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27248-orathaic","name":"orathaic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27248_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1032"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-03-16T16:48:13-07:00","id":1532738,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>orathaic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1532728-soul-mechanics-and-religious-mages?p=1532729#post1532729\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No, I'm not sure what the issue is.<br /> <br /> The game is not the beliefs of Mormons or Jains, it is a game setting. I don't expect things to work in it the way anyone believes it works in reality.<br /> <br /> Also, what is the contradiction you see? Nobody knows what happens to souls when a person dies.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think the main issue is that it would render role-playing such characters difficult. Though 2e tried to move the Obrimos Path away from religious archetypes, they are still associated with the Obrimos Path in the text, and so one has to wonder how exactly they are supposed to role-play such characters.<br /> <br /> As for your question, there a few main problems at hand. One of the major ones is that souls can be destroyed in the setting. According to the doctrine of the Mormons and the Jains, that is impossible, and so in the setting those religions are objectively incorrect. It is also mentioned that Supernal magic cannot create souls, though it is unclear whether that is possible at all. I do not quite recall if the books actually suggested the souls have an origin, which would allow one to play a Mormon or a Jain without being objectively incorrect in the setting. In both of those religions, the soul in uncreated. Another problem is one that is more specific to Jains. The nature of souls being different between beings is something that is not present in Jain doctrine, but it is present in-game, as seen in how various supernatural beings are noted to have different spiritual principles. <br /> <br /> Also, this is not just about those two religions in particular. There are plenty of other religions whose doctrines would not fit with some of the mechanics present in the game. I mainly bring up the question as it can bring up issues at the table. If someone comes in with an idea for a character, only to realize that their character will have to abandon their previous religion two sessions in, for example, it is a practical concern.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28352-verlan","name":"Verlan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"118"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-03-16T19:34:25-07:00","id":1532746,"title":"","content":"Geist has a historical setting in its 2e corebook where the local dead culture has to deal with the fact that the Islamic faith of that place and time does not believe in ghosts, mostly by either focusing on the practicalities or styling themselves as a different sort of being.<br /> <br /> I should imagine a sufficiently dedicated religious monster, particularly of the sort whose notable factions include &quot;there ought to be a religion exclusively for people who can do True magic because Sleepers get everything wrong,&quot; can come up with a reason why the things mages and other monsters tend to describe as a soul does not appear to exhibit the qualities ascribed to the soul in the religious texts available to those still susceptible to the Lie.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-03-16T19:36:03-07:00","id":1532747,"title":"","content":"Mages Awaken to the fact of the Lie and that much of their world is comprised of it, and there's plenty of reason to believe the religions of the world, if ever any of them held truth, very clear don't have all of on this side of the Path. Dedication to religions after the fact often don't have much rooting in the fact that they believe it so much as they can draw power from it. Any reasoning beyond that is a matter for individual mages to deal with.<br /> <br /> Speaking as an ex-Latter Day Saint, It's not particularly difficult to roleplay. You thought you knew everything, then you learn better, you decide if you can keep lying to yourself or cut what is good and useful from what wasn't, and then move forward a wiser person. Mages already do that regularly-religion's just another thing you you awaken to the reality of.<br /> <br /> This is revelation: when the angels turn out to be bastards, they cannot once again be angels.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-03-16T21:55:54-07:00","id":1532753,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1532728-soul-mechanics-and-religious-mages?p=1532747#post1532747\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Mages Awaken to the fact of the Lie and that much of their world is comprised of it, and there's plenty of reason to believe the religions of the world, if ever any of them held truth, very clear don't have all of on this side of the Path. Dedication to religions after the fact often don't have much rooting in the fact that they believe it so much as they can draw power from it. Any reasoning beyond that is a matter for individual mages to deal with.<br /> <br /> Speaking as an ex-Latter Day Saint, It's not particularly difficult to roleplay. You thought you knew everything, then you learn better, you decide if you can keep lying to yourself or cut what is good and useful from what wasn't, and then move forward a wiser person. Mages already do that regularly-religion's just another thing you you awaken to the reality of.<br /> <br /> This is revelation: when the angels turn out to be bastards, they cannot once again be angels.</div> </div> </div> </div>Beautifully put Kells.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","name":"Nicolas Milioni","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4621_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-03-16T23:54:23-07:00","id":1532760,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verlan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1532728-soul-mechanics-and-religious-mages?p=1532738#post1532738\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think the main issue is that it would render role-playing such characters difficult.</div> </div> </div> </div>Roleplaying such characters <i>is</i> difficult.<br /> <br /> In addition to ArcaneArts' excellently put post, I would add that the Awakening doesn't select for the type of personality that would adopt an uncritically doctrinaire religious worldview. That's not the same as saying it selects for the irreligious. It selects for people who are unsatisfied with simple and received wisdom, who are inclined to look for deeper intricacies and unexplored nuance. Any Awakened sorcerer is going to be intrinsically heterodox in their spiritual outlook. I mean, not every religion is closed to the possibility of a Supernal World, but it changes one's view of the universe heavily nonetheless.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/522-stupid-loserman","name":"Stupid Loserman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar522_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1824"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-03-17T02:06:03-07:00","id":1532762,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verlan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1532728-soul-mechanics-and-religious-mages?p=1532738#post1532738\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think the main issue is that it would render role-playing such characters difficult.<br /> &lt;Snip&gt;<br /> As for your question, there a few main problems at hand. One of the major ones is that souls can be destroyed in the setting. According to the doctrine of the Mormons and the Jains, that is impossible...</div> </div> </div> </div>First, having a mistake like this in doctrine is something a character can easily rationalise.<br /> <br /> Souls can not usually be destroyed without magic. One Mage might decided that Mages using magic is Godly and believing God is omnipotent that awakening has brought them closer to God, another might decide that destruction of souls is Demonic and by implication that all mages are against God - becoming a Banisher.<br /> <br /> A third Mage might decide that is something wrong with the description which mages use for souls, and that they aren't what their religion is talking about (after all souls are indestructable) and search for True Souls.<br /> <br /> A fourth Mage might decide their entire religion is built on the lie and used by the Seers to enforce the Tyranny of the Exarchs on the world - becoming a crusader against The Father.<br /> <br /> A fifth Mage might decide they have true insight into the nature of their religion and create a sect within to lead people towards the Truth. Summoning Supernal Beings as part of regular rituals to 'prove' the have God on their side...<br /> <br /> I see this less as a problem and more as an opportunity. But ultimately Mage is a gnositic game of Truths about the world, if you don't like some of those truths you are free to modify them at your table.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":27248,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27248-orathaic","user":"orathaic","date":".  typos 03-17-2024, 05:38 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27248-orathaic","name":"orathaic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27248_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1032"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-03-17T02:52:56-07:00","id":1532763,"title":"","content":"I would point out that Tome of the Pentacle presents the mages of the parts of the world where the Diamond originated <i>also</i> believe the soul can't actually be destroyed, and anything that looks like it's doing that is just harnessing some of its energy as it departs the world.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-03-17T10:48:37-07:00","id":1532770,"title":"","content":"Thank you for your responses, everyone. I think many of those provide workable solutions for the problem at the table. I will admit that personal taste might be a factor here. I know that in both editions of Mage: The Awakening, Awakened mages are depicted as being very separate from thaumaturges, or humans with Supernatural Merits.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28352-verlan","name":"Verlan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"118"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-03-17T10:50:32-07:00","id":1532771,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1532728-soul-mechanics-and-religious-mages?p=1532763#post1532763\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I would point out that Tome of the Pentacle presents the mages of the parts of the world where the Diamond originated <i>also</i> believe the soul can't actually be destroyed, and anything that looks like it's doing that is just harnessing some of its energy as it departs the world.</div> </div> </div> </div>I had not known that, actually. Thank you for pointing that out.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28352-verlan","name":"Verlan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"118"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-03-17T18:54:35-07:00","id":1532788,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verlan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1532728-soul-mechanics-and-religious-mages?p=1532771#post1532771\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I had not known that, actually. Thank you for pointing that out.</div> </div> </div> </div>Tome of the Pentacle might be useful in general for a few angles on how mages can bring different perspectives, theories, and traditions to create variations on what might be considered the baseline of beliefs in any of the three sects.<br /> <br /> As far as the religious qualities of Obrimos go, personally I feel as though Second Edition actually went deeper with that; between the core book and Signs of Sorcery, there's a bit more elaboration on how the symbols of the Aether can be perceived in terms of celestial hierarchies that can be engaged with for purposes of cosmic manipulation or deeper insight into spiritual truths. It's not actually necessary for all Obrimos to be personally religious even when they see things in these terms (and it's said that many dispense with that symbolism and the angels are purely abstracted into scientific principles); I'd say the important thing can be conceptualising reality in terms of a system of hierarchy, of interlocking rational systems driven by higher purpose.<br /> <br /> But for those Obrimos who are personally very religious, they have a lot to work with.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-03-17T21:11:15-07:00","id":1532795,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1532728-soul-mechanics-and-religious-mages?p=1532788#post1532788\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Tome of the Pentacle might be useful in general for a few angles on how mages can bring different perspectives, theories, and traditions to create variations on what might be considered the baseline of beliefs in any of the three sects.<br /> <br /> As far as the religious qualities of Obrimos go, personally I feel as though Second Edition actually went deeper with that; between the core book and Signs of Sorcery, there's a bit more elaboration on how the symbols of the Aether can be perceived in terms of celestial hierarchies that can be engaged with for purposes of cosmic manipulation or deeper insight into spiritual truths. It's not actually necessary for all Obrimos to be personally religious even when they see things in these terms (and it's said that many dispense with that symbolism and the angels are purely abstracted into scientific principles); I'd say the important thing can be conceptualising reality in terms of a system of hierarchy, of interlocking rational systems driven by higher purpose.<br /> <br /> But for those Obrimos who are personally very religious, they have a lot to work with.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I do recall how 2e expanded the domain of what the Obrimos Path covered. I am not entirely sure if hierarchy is a necessary element of the Path's philosophy, but the Path does lend itself towards mechanistic models of the universe's functioning. It does seem reasonable that the Orders and individual mages hold more esoteric doctrines that simply never appear in the books due to the fact that they would rarely be relevant in play. I think the Eyes of Ain Soph are a good example of this. Their write-up in 1e mentioned that they focused on unmanifest existence, which does not quite match up with the standard Mage: The Awakening model where the Supernal is the highest form of existence.<br /> <br /> And yeah, you are correct on your last point.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28352,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28352-verlan","user":"Verlan","date":". 03-18-2024, 12:18 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28352-verlan","name":"Verlan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"118"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-09T09:24:46-07:00","id":1533891,"title":"","content":"A little late to the party, but it seems pretty clear to me that what the Awakened call a soul is not the same thing these religions call a soul. In the setting, a \u201csoul\u201d is not the sole vehicle of a person\u2019s self. Having your soul removed doesn\u2019t immediately remove your personality and volition, and you can even swap souls with basically no side effects. So, by my reading, \u201csouls\u201d aren\u2019t even unique eschatological phenomena. They\u2019re more an interchangeable medium that merely plugs the person\u2019s actual \u201cself\u201d into their mortal form. Removing it is like cutting out parts of a person\u2019s brain: it severely inhibits the person\u2019s ability to function, but it\u2019s not actually relevant in an eschatological sense.<br /> The fact that the hypothetical \u201csoul soul\u201d isn\u2019t detectable or manipulatable by Supernal magic might only reinforce the religious view of souls as inviolate and eternal.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#000080\">The longer I study science the more I am convinced that it is <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\">functionally indistinguishable from what our ancestors would refer to as sorcery</span>. And I would know, being both scientist and sorcerer.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23730-scriptorian","name":"Scriptorian","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23730_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"181"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-09T13:20:25-07:00","id":1533899,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Scriptorian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1532728-soul-mechanics-and-religious-mages?p=1533891#post1533891\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A little late to the party, but it seems pretty clear to me that what the Awakened call a soul is not the same thing these religions call a soul. In the setting, a \u201csoul\u201d is not the sole vehicle of a person\u2019s self. Having your soul removed doesn\u2019t immediately remove your personality and volition, and you can even swap souls with basically no side effects. So, by my reading, \u201csouls\u201d aren\u2019t even unique eschatological phenomena. They\u2019re more an interchangeable medium that merely plugs the person\u2019s actual \u201cself\u201d into their mortal form. Removing it is like cutting out parts of a person\u2019s brain: it severely inhibits the person\u2019s ability to function, but it\u2019s not actually relevant in an eschatological sense.<br /> The fact that the hypothetical \u201csoul soul\u201d isn\u2019t detectable or manipulatable by Supernal magic might only reinforce the religious view of souls as inviolate and eternal.</div> </div> </div> </div>As others have said before, I think that it is pretty easy to see individual mages using that sort of reasoning in regards to the problem. I think that the larger question then is why the books use the term soul to refer to an animating essence in the first place. In general, I feel like the established cosmology of the setting tends to overshadow the worldviews of individual mages. The core rulebook exemplifies this pretty well. In the beginning, the book clearly differentiates mortal magic from Supernal magic, and yet the book also constantly references mortal religious traditions when it speaks of mages. Also, Awakening does not really do a good job of establishing that what the game's mechanics refer to as a soul might not match philosophical models of the soul. For example, the whole philosophy of the Tremere in 2e is reliant upon the idea that they really do strip themselves of their souls, and are thus able to identify with a monistic world-soul. The Tremere very much interact with the soul presented by the game's mechanics. If anything, the Awakened should be very aware of the fact that what seem like distant, abstract questions are actually empirical in nature.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28352-verlan","name":"Verlan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"118"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-09T19:41:19-07:00","id":1533924,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verlan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1532728-soul-mechanics-and-religious-mages?p=1533899#post1533899\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think that the larger question then is why the books use the term soul to refer to an animating essence in the first place.</div> </div> </div> </div>Because the things that constitute a soul \u2014 the things the Subtle Arcana govern \u2014 include among them the consciousness, the animating essence, the capacity for emotional resonance, the predispositions of one's role in the world, and the parts of oneself that linger after death (including character flaws and moral failings).<br /> <br /> When removing the package deal that these things correlate to causes a character to be given over to their personal weaknesses, more susceptible to being overtaken by possessing entities, and cut off from their inner landscape, eventually losing their inclination to act on their own initiative, that tends to check a lot of people's boxes for &quot;has this person Lost Something Spiritually Important?&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In general, I feel like the established cosmology of the setting tends to overshadow the worldviews of individual mages. </div> </div> </div>Because the books are mainly written to provide the established cosmology of the setting first and example characters second, since they're mostly tools for troupes to create characters and local settings for their own stories.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The core rulebook exemplifies this pretty well. In the beginning, the book clearly differentiates mortal magic from Supernal magic, and yet the book also constantly references mortal religious traditions when it speaks of mages. </div> </div> </div>Because many mortal religious traditions have magical traditions that inform mages' backgrounds and inform their use of magic the way literally anything else does. Religion is a symbol-dense subject and magic is the leveraging of symbolism to have concrete effects upon the world.<br /> <br /> It is distinctly possible I have misunderstood your meaning here, in which case I would ask you to explain what you mean by &quot;constantly referencing mortal religious traditions&quot; and &quot;clearly differentiating mortal magic from Supernal magic.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, Awakening does not really do a good job of establishing that what the game's mechanics refer to as a soul might not match philosophical models of the soul. For example, the whole philosophy of the Tremere in 2e is reliant upon the idea that they really do strip themselves of their souls, and are thus able to identify with a monistic world-soul. The Tremere very much interact with the soul presented by the game's mechanics. If anything, the Awakened should be very aware of the fact that what seem like distant, abstract questions are actually empirical in nature. </div> </div> </div>The Tremere are the subject-matter experts in souls among the Awakened. They are also a bunch of evil bastards who socially poisoned the well when their unsavory activities were found out along with taking most of their findings in the divorce from the Diamond. The Tremere are so far underground that the fact they're still an organized group is not common knowledge. &quot;The Awakened should know this hidden lore about how souls work&quot; is absolutely not true in the modern setting as-presented specifically because the premier example of a Reaper in the world is &quot;those monsters who were cannibalizing soul-related Legacies behind our backs when we worked with them centuries ago and who still show up every now and then making trouble by stealing people's souls, a thing any mage with a moderate amount of Death lore can also do.&quot;<br /> <br /> Separate from that, the Tremere are also attaching ideology to the fact that the Hollow where their soul used to be allows them to do magic and physically interact with souls without specific Arcana in ways that gel with their disdain for the inhuman. They don't have any insight on how a hypothetical afterlife works that any other mage couldn't stumble upon, nor what normally happens to the soul after death; they don't know if werewolves go to Hell or if there's any actual benefits to high Integrity beyond those evidenced in life, and the distinction between &quot;an individual human soul is a discrete object that happens to be internally connected to every other human soul through the Astral&quot; and &quot;the Astral demonstrates that every individual soul is a branch of the world-soul&quot; is so fine as to be philosophical.<br /> <br /> Again, it is possible I have misunderstood your meaning, in which case I would ask for examples of these &quot;distant, abstract questions&quot; that are supposedly actually empirical.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-10T01:08:11-07:00","id":1533941,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verlan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1532728-soul-mechanics-and-religious-mages?p=1533899#post1533899\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Tremere very much interact with the soul presented by the game's mechanics. If anything, the Awakened should be very aware of the fact that what seem like distant, abstract questions are actually empirical in nature.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think you might've misunderstood something here (or I might just misunderstand you). Tremere transform themselves in ways that affect the soul as presented by game mechanics. That they have religious reasons for doing so that doesn't prove anything regarding the nature of souls as presented by religions.<br /> <br /> <br /> As an aside, the core book presents the setting as it is seen and known from the persective of the Pentacle (and more specifically, the Diamond). Since the Diamond orders are religious in nature, you kinda <i>have</i> to adjust your pre-Awakening religion to fit in at least some ways. (Too bad your Sleeper peers doesn't have your new info about how the world really works. It's probably the result of Seers/the Lie meddling in your religion, corrupting how its taught to the Sleeping masses.)<br /> If you're not ready to do that, and you're not snatched and indoctrinated by Seers, you'll either find some grouping within the Free Council which reaffirms your beliefs, or not join any of the named orders.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer for <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/410713/Bloodlines-The-Ageless\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Ageless</a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/491569/Fire--Revolution-Carthians\" target=\"_blank\">Fire &amp; Revolution: Carthians</a> on STV<br /> Some other stuff I've done: Ordo Dracul Mysteries: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1138490-idea-coil-of-owls?p=1140522\" target=\"_blank\">Mystery of Smoke</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1227848-mystery-of-%C5%BEiva-house-rule-revision\" target=\"_blank\">Revised Mystery of \u017diva</a> Mage The Awakening: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B2LMDRtfCcpdZDU1dWJzT1dNeEE/view\" target=\"_blank\">Spell Quick Reference</a> (single page and landscape for computer screens)","edited":{"userid":11887,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","user":"Tessie","date":". 04-10-2024, 04:10 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","name":"Tessie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11887_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-10T13:29:31-07:00","id":1533995,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1532728-soul-mechanics-and-religious-mages?p=1533924#post1533924\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Because the things that constitute a soul \u2014 the things the Subtle Arcana govern \u2014 include among them the consciousness, the animating essence, the capacity for emotional resonance, the predispositions of one's role in the world, and the parts of oneself that linger after death (including character flaws and moral failings).<br /> <br /> When removing the package deal that these things correlate to causes a character to be given over to their personal weaknesses, more susceptible to being overtaken by possessing entities, and cut off from their inner landscape, eventually losing their inclination to act on their own initiative, that tends to check a lot of people's boxes for &quot;has this person Lost Something Spiritually Important?&quot;<br /> <br /> Because the books are mainly written to provide the established cosmology of the setting first and example characters second, since they're mostly tools for troupes to create characters and local settings for their own stories.<br /> <br /> Because many mortal religious traditions have magical traditions that inform mages' backgrounds and inform their use of magic the way literally anything else does. Religion is a symbol-dense subject and magic is the leveraging of symbolism to have concrete effects upon the world.<br /> <br /> It is distinctly possible I have misunderstood your meaning here, in which case I would ask you to explain what you mean by &quot;constantly referencing mortal religious traditions&quot; and &quot;clearly differentiating mortal magic from Supernal magic.&quot;<br /> <br /> The Tremere are the subject-matter experts in souls among the Awakened. They are also a bunch of evil bastards who socially poisoned the well when their unsavory activities were found out along with taking most of their findings in the divorce from the Diamond. The Tremere are so far underground that the fact they're still an organized group is not common knowledge. &quot;The Awakened should know this hidden lore about how souls work&quot; is absolutely not true in the modern setting as-presented specifically because the premier example of a Reaper in the world is &quot;those monsters who were cannibalizing soul-related Legacies behind our backs when we worked with them centuries ago and who still show up every now and then making trouble by stealing people's souls, a thing any mage with a moderate amount of Death lore can also do.&quot;<br /> <br /> Separate from that, the Tremere are also attaching ideology to the fact that the Hollow where their soul used to be allows them to do magic and physically interact with souls without specific Arcana in ways that gel with their disdain for the inhuman. They don't have any insight on how a hypothetical afterlife works that any other mage couldn't stumble upon, nor what normally happens to the soul after death; they don't know if werewolves go to Hell or if there's any actual benefits to high Integrity beyond those evidenced in life, and the distinction between &quot;an individual human soul is a discrete object that happens to be internally connected to every other human soul through the Astral&quot; and &quot;the Astral demonstrates that every individual soul is a branch of the world-soul&quot; is so fine as to be philosophical.<br /> <br /> Again, it is possible I have misunderstood your meaning, in which case I would ask for examples of these &quot;distant, abstract questions&quot; that are supposedly actually empirical.</div> </div> </div> </div>In 1e, the tradition of Awakened magic is presented as something that completes the fragmentary traditions of mortal magicians. It is presented as something distinct from mortal magic, but it is also presented as something that is more complete than it. In 2e, the book just states that Supernal magic is distinct from the magic of mortal occultists. In regards to the matter of empiricism, there are multiple rotes that specifically relate to the afterlife. For example, a mage with Death 5 can communicate with the deceased and gain information from them. As for the Tremere, the fact remains that they are presented as a Legacy that primarily revolves around stealing souls to extend one's lifespan. By even manifesting the statement that they are a soul-stealers, one is implicitly holding to a philosophical assumption, that being that whatever the Tremere are stealing are actually souls. If one is going by the reasoning that the Awakened disagree on what souls actually are, then not all mages should even consider the Tremere to be soul-stealers.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28352-verlan","name":"Verlan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"118"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-10T13:34:43-07:00","id":1533996,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tessie</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1532728-soul-mechanics-and-religious-mages?p=1533941#post1533941\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think you might've misunderstood something here (or I might just misunderstand you). Tremere transform themselves in ways that affect the soul as presented by game mechanics. That they have religious reasons for doing so that doesn't prove anything regarding the nature of souls as presented by religions.<br /> <br /> <br /> As an aside, the core book presents the setting as it is seen and known from the persective of the Pentacle (and more specifically, the Diamond). Since the Diamond orders are religious in nature, you kinda <i>have</i> to adjust your pre-Awakening religion to fit in at least some ways. (Too bad your Sleeper peers doesn't have your new info about how the world really works. It's probably the result of Seers/the Lie meddling in your religion, corrupting how its taught to the Sleeping masses.)<br /> If you're not ready to do that, and you're not snatched and indoctrinated by Seers, you'll either find some grouping within the Free Council which reaffirms your beliefs, or not join any of the named orders.</div> </div> </div> </div>The thing is that the Free Council itself has its own philosophy that I do not think all mages who would involve themselves with mortal religion would agree with. The Free Council explicitly supports the idea that the Fallen World has its own magic, and that human innovation can essentially pierce a path to the Supernal. Some mages might agree with it, but I can think of plenty of examples where a mage would hold their worldview to be the objective truth. In philosophy, such individuals would likely have more common with the Mysterium, given that Order's focus on revelation. However, the Mysterium itself largely adheres to the magical theory of the Diamond. Thus, a mage whose magical worldview is influenced more by mortal occultism may not seek to join the Mysterium.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28352-verlan","name":"Verlan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"118"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-10T14:09:02-07:00","id":1533999,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verlan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1532728-soul-mechanics-and-religious-mages?p=1533995#post1533995\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In 1e, the tradition of Awakened magic is presented as something that completes the fragmentary traditions of mortal magicians. It is presented as something distinct from mortal magic, but it is also presented as something that is more complete than it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, that's the recurring &quot;the blind men and the elephant&quot; bit where various traditions have parts of the big picture that they got right, which naturally entails those same various traditions getting a bunch of stuff <i>wrong</i>.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In regards to the matter of empiricism, there are multiple rotes that specifically relate to the afterlife. For example, a mage with Death 5 can communicate with the deceased and gain information from them. </div> </div> </div>You are referring to spells that talk to ghosts. Ghosts are neither souls nor the people they are spawned from, nor do they dwell in the afterlife nor have any special insight into what awaits them when they are able to pass on \u2014 that same Mastery of Death allows you to <i>create ghosts of people who aren't dead</i>.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As for the Tremere, the fact remains that they are presented as a Legacy that primarily revolves around stealing souls to extend one's lifespan. By even manifesting the statement that they are a soul-stealers, one is implicitly holding to a philosophical assumption, that being that whatever the Tremere are stealing are actually souls. If one is going by the reasoning that the Awakened disagree on what souls actually are, then not all mages should even consider the Tremere to be soul-stealers. </div> </div> </div>The thing the Tremere steal is a consistent thing whose loss has consistent effects and wizards tend to give names to things that they deal with. The Awakened can argue terminology without the pretense that removing the thing the game system refers to as a soul is not harmful at a level most sociable mages would not support.<br /> <br /> Again, this is the thing that allows you to maintain your self-image, continue to improve yourself, stave off possession, refresh your reserves of determination without guilt, and tap into the worlds of imagination and communion with the wider world. If you have a singular term for all that, I'm eager to hear it, but &quot;the Tremere remove an invisible part of the human package that sustains one's autonomy and spiritual health to feed their own immortality and worldly power&quot; does not have to mesh with every religion's definition of the soul to be a grave offense upon another person for which the scions of the Seventh Dragon are rightly reviled.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-10T20:51:47-07:00","id":1534009,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1532728-soul-mechanics-and-religious-mages?p=1533999#post1533999\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes, that's the recurring &quot;the blind men and the elephant&quot; bit where various traditions have parts of the big picture that they got right, which naturally entails those same various traditions getting a bunch of stuff <i>wrong</i>.<br /> <br /> You are referring to spells that talk to ghosts. Ghosts are neither souls nor the people they are spawned from, nor do they dwell in the afterlife nor have any special insight into what awaits them when they are able to pass on \u2014 that same Mastery of Death allows you to <i>create ghosts of people who aren't dead</i>.<br /> <br /> The thing the Tremere steal is a consistent thing whose loss has consistent effects and wizards tend to give names to things that they deal with. The Awakened can argue terminology without the pretense that removing the thing the game system refers to as a soul is not harmful at a level most sociable mages would not support.<br /> <br /> Again, this is the thing that allows you to maintain your self-image, continue to improve yourself, stave off possession, refresh your reserves of determination without guilt, and tap into the worlds of imagination and communion with the wider world. If you have a singular term for all that, I'm eager to hear it, but &quot;the Tremere remove an invisible part of the human package that sustains one's autonomy and spiritual health to feed their own immortality and worldly power&quot; does not have to mesh with every religion's definition of the soul to be a grave offense upon another person for which the scions of the Seventh Dragon are rightly reviled.</div> </div> </div> </div>In regards to mortal magical traditions, I used the example from 1e as an illustration of the patronizing attitude that Awakening has towards religion and occultism. One cannot coherently play a mage who is wholly invested in a given non-Awakened magical theory; the supremacy of the game's in-built cosmology is always established.<br /> <br /> In regards to the example with Death 5, I was referring to Death 5 as presented in 1e. The core rulebook for 1e was released before Geist: The Sin-Eater was released, and the ontology of ghosts presented by Geist cannot be reasonably held to apply to the example that I gave. Furthermore, several parts of the core rulebook for 1e address matters that 2e has not addressed, such as the manner in which souls fall into matter.<br /> <br /> Finally, I did not say that mages did not have a good reason to revile the Tremere. I just stated not all mages would have a reason to consider the Tremere to be soul-stealers.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28352-verlan","name":"Verlan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"118"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-11T00:44:24-07:00","id":1534018,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verlan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1532728-soul-mechanics-and-religious-mages?p=1533995#post1533995\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In regards to the matter of empiricism, there are multiple rotes that specifically relate to the afterlife. For example, a mage with Death 5 can communicate with the deceased and gain information from them.</div> </div> </div> </div>Death 2 is enough for that (or Death 3 if you specifically want to communicate with ghosts from the Underworld), but at the same time there's no evidence that the information received comes from the actual afterlife. The Underworld is where <i>ghosts</i> end up, and empirical studies easily proves that ghosts are <i>not</i> a true continuation of the person, since they can be created without someone dying, both by Death 5 as mentioned, and from near death experiences where the actual person survived (which actually allows multiple ghosts to spawn from the same person). Also, a lot of people (most, I think, but I don't quite remember if it's ever spelled out explicitly) don't even leave ghosts behind when they die.<br /> <br /> On top of that, the soul (as presented by the game and fully visible to Mage Sight) departs dead bodies in a process separate from how ghosts are formed. Many mages believe they return to Stygia, but where they end up is actually not known.<br /> <br /> All of this would empirically lead to the conclusion that ghosts are psychic imprints or echoes left behind. If there's an actual afterlife, it's almost certainly not the Underworld.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As for the Tremere, the fact remains that they are presented as a Legacy that primarily revolves around stealing souls to extend one's lifespan. By even manifesting the statement that they are a soul-stealers, one is implicitly holding to a philosophical assumption, that being that whatever the Tremere are stealing are actually souls. If one is going by the reasoning that the Awakened disagree on what souls actually are, then not all mages should even consider the Tremere to be soul-stealers. </div> </div> </div>That's just consistent terminology. I have no idea why you're focusing on the Tremere. They're not more or less significant than any other mention of souls in the setting.<br /> A\u200b\u200b\u200bnd no, not all mages might consider the Tremere to be actual soul stealers. But most of the player-presenting mages will, on account of them belonging to religious groupings that has established views about the subject.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The thing is that the Free Council itself has its own philosophy that I do not think all mages who would involve themselves with mortal religion would agree with. </div> </div> </div>The Free Council is also a lot less monolithic than any of the other orders, by virtue of consisting of a huge amount of previously nameless orders. I'm certain there are many groupings within the Free Council that believe <i>their</i> magical tradition/religion is the actual correct one, or that are open to individual members who want to prove that theirs is the correct in order to find out which, if any in particular, holds more true than the others.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer for <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/410713/Bloodlines-The-Ageless\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Ageless</a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/491569/Fire--Revolution-Carthians\" target=\"_blank\">Fire &amp; Revolution: Carthians</a> on STV<br /> Some other stuff I've done: Ordo Dracul Mysteries: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1138490-idea-coil-of-owls?p=1140522\" target=\"_blank\">Mystery of Smoke</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1227848-mystery-of-%C5%BEiva-house-rule-revision\" target=\"_blank\">Revised Mystery of \u017diva</a> Mage The Awakening: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B2LMDRtfCcpdZDU1dWJzT1dNeEE/view\" target=\"_blank\">Spell Quick Reference</a> (single page and landscape for computer screens)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","name":"Tessie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11887_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-11T11:01:44-07:00","id":1534034,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tessie</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1532728-soul-mechanics-and-religious-mages?p=1534018#post1534018\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Death 2 is enough for that (or Death 3 if you specifically want to communicate with ghosts from the Underworld), but at the same time there's no evidence that the information received comes from the actual afterlife. The Underworld is where <i>ghosts</i> end up, and empirical studies easily proves that ghosts are <i>not</i> a true continuation of the person, since they can be created without someone dying, both by Death 5 as mentioned, and from near death experiences where the actual person survived (which actually allows multiple ghosts to spawn from the same person). Also, a lot of people (most, I think, but I don't quite remember if it's ever spelled out explicitly) don't even leave ghosts behind when they die.<br /> <br /> On top of that, the soul (as presented by the game and fully visible to Mage Sight) departs dead bodies in a process separate from how ghosts are formed. Many mages believe they return to Stygia, but where they end up is actually not known.<br /> <br /> All of this would empirically lead to the conclusion that ghosts are psychic imprints or echoes left behind. If there's an actual afterlife, it's almost certainly not the Underworld.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> That's just consistent terminology. I have no idea why you're focusing on the Tremere. They're not more or less significant than any other mention of souls in the setting.<br /> A\u200b\u200bnd no, not all mages might consider the Tremere to be actual soul stealers. But most of the player-presenting mages will, on account of them belonging to religious groupings that has established views about the subject.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> The Free Council is also a lot less monolithic than any of the other orders, by virtue of consisting of a huge amount of previously nameless orders. I'm certain there are many groupings within the Free Council that believe <i>their</i> magical tradition/religion is the actual correct one, or that are open to individual members who want to prove that theirs is the correct in order to find out which, if any in particular, holds more true than the others.</div> </div> </div> </div>In regards to the matter of ghosts, I was referring to how the Death Arcanum is presented in 1e. The Underworld is mentioned, but a specific ontology of ghosts is not provided. Instead, the nature of ghosts is presented as ambiguous. <br /> <br /> In general, it just feels as though the books pay lip service to how individual views vary across mages. Much of the time, the Diamond's magical theories are presented as more accurate than any of the alternatives. Furthermore, by the nature of the game, the cosmology of the Supernal tends to take precedence. Any given magical theory will have to take that into account. Due to this, the magical theories of the Awakened are the ones that are most discussed in the text, and the text is not always effective at separating the worldviews of mages from what is objectively accurate.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28352-verlan","name":"Verlan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"118"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-11T11:35:59-07:00","id":1534037,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verlan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1532728-soul-mechanics-and-religious-mages?p=1534009#post1534009\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In regards to mortal magical traditions, I used the example from 1e as an illustration of the patronizing attitude that Awakening has towards religion and occultism. One cannot coherently play a mage who is wholly invested in a given non-Awakened magical theory; the supremacy of the game's in-built cosmology is always established.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes. Because this is a game whose protagonists have to contend with the fact that they are isolated from the rest of humanity by knowing and having the means of learning things that cannot be shared, as well as the fact that they and their peers often become hubristic egotists in the face of their own power. Cultural traditions in this context are only ever going to have the importance the characters place on them as people with magical insight into the workings of the world.<br /> <br /> You cannot have a coherent metaphysic where souls are a source of occult power related to an objective and ineffable Truth behind reality and also say that every religion's definition of the soul is correctly referring to the same thing in that setting when religion itself is not inherently magical.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In regards to the example with Death 5, I was referring to Death 5 as presented in 1e. The core rulebook for 1e was released before Geist: The Sin-Eater was released, and the ontology of ghosts presented by Geist cannot be reasonably held to apply to the example that I gave. Furthermore, several parts of the core rulebook for 1e address matters that 2e has not addressed, such as the manner in which souls fall into matter. </div> </div> </div>The year is 2024. Geist has existed in 1e form for nearly fifteen years and 2e form for nearly six. Mage 2e has been around for eight years. Both editions of Mage came out before their respective editions nailed down the Underworld, and ghosts of nonhuman things (such as literal ghost towns or the animal ghosts of the Dreaming Earth) pre-date Geist sufficiently to underline the fact that a ghost is not a soul (discounting 1e's own writeup on the Death Arcanum explicitly drawing a distinction between ghosts and souls).<br /> <br /> Even without all of this, the books have literally never presented ghosts in their typical contexts as occupying an otherwise inaccessible afterlife; the unquiet dead are consistently rendered in terms suitable to an occult horror setting, with necromancy's capacity for providing information relating more generally to the fact that the dead remember things from when they were alive, can lurk unseen, and have magic powers that can tell them what they don't already know.<br /> <br /> It's a Gnostic game that draws some of its concepts and terminology from Buddhism. Being able to talk to ghosts doesn't tell you anything about the afterlife just because they're invisible and semi-immortal. This has always been the case.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Finally, I did not say that mages did not have a good reason to revile the Tremere. I just stated not all mages would have a reason to consider the Tremere to be soul-stealers. </div> </div> </div>By what practical metric would you consider this to be a meaningful distinction without defining what the suitable term is for the thing the Tremere take out of people?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-12T12:17:24-07:00","id":1534103,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1532728-soul-mechanics-and-religious-mages?p=1534037#post1534037\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes. Because this is a game whose protagonists have to contend with the fact that they are isolated from the rest of humanity by knowing and having the means of learning things that cannot be shared, as well as the fact that they and their peers often become hubristic egotists in the face of their own power. Cultural traditions in this context are only ever going to have the importance the characters place on them as people with magical insight into the workings of the world.<br /> <br /> You cannot have a coherent metaphysic where souls are a source of occult power related to an objective and ineffable Truth behind reality and also say that every religion's definition of the soul is correctly referring to the same thing in that setting when religion itself is not inherently magical.<br /> <br /> The year is 2024. Geist has existed in 1e form for nearly fifteen years and 2e form for nearly six. Mage 2e has been around for eight years. Both editions of Mage came out before their respective editions nailed down the Underworld, and ghosts of nonhuman things (such as literal ghost towns or the animal ghosts of the Dreaming Earth) pre-date Geist sufficiently to underline the fact that a ghost is not a soul (discounting 1e's own writeup on the Death Arcanum explicitly drawing a distinction between ghosts and souls).<br /> <br /> Even without all of this, the books have literally never presented ghosts in their typical contexts as occupying an otherwise inaccessible afterlife; the unquiet dead are consistently rendered in terms suitable to an occult horror setting, with necromancy's capacity for providing information relating more generally to the fact that the dead remember things from when they were alive, can lurk unseen, and have magic powers that can tell them what they don't already know.<br /> <br /> It's a Gnostic game that draws some of its concepts and terminology from Buddhism. Being able to talk to ghosts doesn't tell you anything about the afterlife just because they're invisible and semi-immortal. This has always been the case.<br /> <br /> By what practical metric would you consider this to be a meaningful distinction without defining what the suitable term is for the thing the Tremere take out of people?</div> </div> </div> </div>Awakening is only Gnostic in regards to epistemology, in that there is a focus on liberating, experiential divine knowledge. By that standard, many religions are Gnostic. The cosmology of the setting itself is not Gnostic. Awakening itself presents its own very specific image of the wizard, and uses terminology from a variety of traditions in order to present it. <br /> <br /> Also, the fact that there are ghosts of non-human things does not necessarily imply that a ghost is not a soul. Inanimate objects and animals have been thought to have souls in some traditions.<br /> <br /> Furthermore, Awakening has presented ghosts in their classic magico-religious contexts. Ghosts have commonly been held to remember things from their life, and they have often been thought to possess supernatural perceptions that render communication with them practically useful.<br /> <br /> At the end of the day, Awakening draws from real-world traditions in an erratic manner in order to manifest its setting, and within that setting, presents such traditions as misguided. Instead, the cosmologies of the Awakened are presented as superior. It would not really matter if the authors were clear that that was their intent, and that they intended no disrespect to the religions that they mined for content, but that is not the case.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28352-verlan","name":"Verlan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"118"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-12T15:44:28-07:00","id":1534111,"title":"","content":"In the matter of ghosts being souls, page 97 of the Mage 2ed core book states that a person\u2019s soul is not their mind, spirit, or the source of their morality. It also states it is not made of ephemera, which ghosts are. Mage Sight (active and focused) draw a clear distinction between ghosts and souls.<br /> <br /> In page 185 of the Geist 2ed core book, there is a tab that specifically mentions ghosts are not souls. Ghosts are created when the powerful death resonance of an event leaves an imprint in Twilight. A minor imprint (Rank 1) is stuck in a loop, a stronger one is closer to the person that was imprinted (Rank 2) and anything beyond are beings affected by the Underworld's Resonance (Geist 2ed core book page 187). The existance of multiple Dopplegangers while a person is alive provides further evidence to differentiate the person from the ghosts they create (Geist 2ed page 196).<br /> <br /> In the subject of the Awakened being always right, page 239 of the Mage 2ed core book has a side tab that points out the opposite. Ultimately the occult is hidden from mundane eyes and no one has a perfect picture, with the different game lines providing infrastructure to build stories on. Exploring how it relates to a religious traditions and how it affects a character is a possible direction for a chronicle. It all depends to what suit the the mood and themes of your chronicles, as well as what your table is comfortable with.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>New experiences are the font of creativity, when seeking inspiration, break your routine.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1278855-the-agathos-kai-sophos-a-acanthus-legacy-of-strategists\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#1155CC\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Agathos Kai Sophos</span></span></a><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, an Acanthus Legacy of strategists (Mind/Time)</span><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1360568-the-szary-stra%C5%BCnik-an-obrimos-legacy-of-scholars-of-the-glyphs-of-fate\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#1155CC\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Szary Stra\u017cnik</span></span></a><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, an Obrimos Legacy whose invisible hands guide through the Glyphs of Fate (Fate/Prime)</span>","edited":{"userid":24280,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24280-kaiserafini","user":"KaiserAfini","date":". 04-12-2024, 06:48 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24280-kaiserafini","name":"KaiserAfini","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24280_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2648"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-12T21:15:50-07:00","id":1534118,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verlan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1532728-soul-mechanics-and-religious-mages?p=1534103#post1534103\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Awakening is only Gnostic in regards to epistemology, in that there is a focus on liberating, experiential divine knowledge. By that standard, many religions are Gnostic. The cosmology of the setting itself is not Gnostic. Awakening itself presents its own very specific image of the wizard, and uses terminology from a variety of traditions in order to present it.</div> </div> </div> </div>My dude, one of the primary antagonist groups in this setting serves the Demiurges who want humanity to remain trapped in spiritual infancy. There is a great gulf of ignorance separating the Truth from experiential reality. Mind/body dualism is more strongly encoded into the standard model for Awakened magic than for many other monsters and the primary counterpoint to sensual pleasures being a distraction from higher pursuits is in ecstatic practices. <i>The world is set up to prevent people from finding out the Truth, to the specific benefit of the bastards that started and sustain this state of affairs.</i> Chronicles and particularly Awakening is a Gnostic setting.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Furthermore, Awakening has presented ghosts in their classic magico-religious contexts. Ghosts have commonly been held to remember things from their life, and they have often been thought to possess supernatural perceptions that render communication with them practically useful. </div> </div> </div>Yes, this is the thing that I just said by way of explaining how being able to talk to ghosts does not mean mages have proof of an afterlife that is not part of the subtranscendent Realms Invisible.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> At the end of the day, Awakening draws from real-world traditions in an erratic manner in order to manifest its setting, and within that setting, presents such traditions as misguided. Instead, the cosmologies of the Awakened are presented as superior. It would not really matter if the authors were clear that that was their intent, and that they intended no disrespect to the religions that they mined for content, but that is not the case. </div> </div> </div>This would be the least bit relevant if Mage: the Awakening made any pretense of being a game specifically about being a practitioner of real-world magical traditions rendered supernaturally efficacious, if the lines released before it were not similarly clear that their take on vampires and werewolves had pronounced differences from at least some subset of folkloric or pop-cultural vampires and werewolves, and if Chronicles as a whole were an All Occult Beliefs Are Unconditionally True setting instead of, y'know, running the spectrum from horror/mystery-flavored urban fantasy to your various subtypes of horror where &quot;alienation&quot; is a theme or subtheme in at least half the gamelines.<br /> <br /> In contrast to Ascension's &quot;everyone starts with a paradigm and as they rise in Arete the training wheels start to come off,&quot; Awakening runs on &quot;everyone can leverage any symbolism they can recognize and that means career wizards are magical magpies who favor the tools they're best at using.&quot; The Diamond is an organization that was created to better understand the magical world as the Awakened experience it, which is not the primary purpose behind the esoteric doctrines of e.g. Mormonism. Mages who encounter a contradiction in terminology between reality and their religious beliefs either synthesize a new understanding of the two or let one fall by the wayside, but either way their own powers are beholden to their own wills and perspectives, which shape the uses they put them to but do not have causative influence on the reality they interact with.<br /> <br /> &quot;Mage doesn't canonize real-world magical traditions as true&quot; is a statement on the same level as &quot;Werewolf isn't a game about playing <i>The Wolf Man</i>&quot; or &quot;Changeling isn't a game about playing the fairy that was left in the crib&quot; or &quot;Demon isn't a game about playing an escapee from Hell.&quot; Chronicles of Darkness games are often built toward the end of playing beings with only partial relation to the common archetype of a given sort of monster, to the point where the two gamelines whose characters are the trickiest to describe are the ones with an intrinsic connection to a specific branch of the setting's cosmology; &quot;the game about investigating all sorts of magical weirdness and Fortean phenomena privileges the perspective of magical researchers powered by the transcendent force that they use to describe reality and the evidence of their magical eyes over the teachings of their nonmagical upbringing that they broke from to become a wizard&quot; doesn't rate.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-13T00:43:00-07:00","id":1534121,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verlan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1532728-soul-mechanics-and-religious-mages?p=1534103#post1534103\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">At the end of the day, Awakening draws from real-world traditions in an erratic manner in order to manifest its setting, and within that setting, presents such traditions as misguided. Instead, the cosmologies of the Awakened are presented as superior. It would not really matter if the authors were clear that that was their intent, and that they intended no disrespect to the religions that they mined for content, but that is not the case.</div> </div> </div> </div>Going two for two (which is two too many), but I'm sorry, I have to ask this question:<br /> <br /> Are you, as the person who took the collected mythology and religious position of cherubim and made them the center of a diaper fetish fan game in a humanist-themed urban-horror mystery roleplaying game setting, saying that you are disappointed that the humanist-themed urban-horror mystery game about wizards doesn't commit to treating occult and religious practices with enough dignity with what I'm reading as the textual equivalent of a straight face?<br /> <br /> This is a rhetorical question I just want to hang on this conversation for framing purposes.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-13T21:14:42-07:00","id":1534178,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1532728-soul-mechanics-and-religious-mages?p=1534118#post1534118\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My dude, one of the primary antagonist groups in this setting serves the Demiurges who want humanity to remain trapped in spiritual infancy. There is a great gulf of ignorance separating the Truth from experiential reality. Mind/body dualism is more strongly encoded into the standard model for Awakened magic than for many other monsters and the primary counterpoint to sensual pleasures being a distraction from higher pursuits is in ecstatic practices. <i>The world is set up to prevent people from finding out the Truth, to the specific benefit of the bastards that started and sustain this state of affairs.</i> Chronicles and particularly Awakening is a Gnostic setting.<br /> <br /> Yes, this is the thing that I just said by way of explaining how being able to talk to ghosts does not mean mages have proof of an afterlife that is not part of the subtranscendent Realms Invisible.<br /> <br /> This would be the least bit relevant if Mage: the Awakening made any pretense of being a game specifically about being a practitioner of real-world magical traditions rendered supernaturally efficacious, if the lines released before it were not similarly clear that their take on vampires and werewolves had pronounced differences from at least some subset of folkloric or pop-cultural vampires and werewolves, and if Chronicles as a whole were an All Occult Beliefs Are Unconditionally True setting instead of, y'know, running the spectrum from horror/mystery-flavored urban fantasy to your various subtypes of horror where &quot;alienation&quot; is a theme or subtheme in at least half the gamelines.<br /> <br /> In contrast to Ascension's &quot;everyone starts with a paradigm and as they rise in Arete the training wheels start to come off,&quot; Awakening runs on &quot;everyone can leverage any symbolism they can recognize and that means career wizards are magical magpies who favor the tools they're best at using.&quot; The Diamond is an organization that was created to better understand the magical world as the Awakened experience it, which is not the primary purpose behind the esoteric doctrines of e.g. Mormonism. Mages who encounter a contradiction in terminology between reality and their religious beliefs either synthesize a new understanding of the two or let one fall by the wayside, but either way their own powers are beholden to their own wills and perspectives, which shape the uses they put them to but do not have causative influence on the reality they interact with.<br /> <br /> &quot;Mage doesn't canonize real-world magical traditions as true&quot; is a statement on the same level as &quot;Werewolf isn't a game about playing <i>The Wolf Man</i>&quot; or &quot;Changeling isn't a game about playing the fairy that was left in the crib&quot; or &quot;Demon isn't a game about playing an escapee from Hell.&quot; Chronicles of Darkness games are often built toward the end of playing beings with only partial relation to the common archetype of a given sort of monster, to the point where the two gamelines whose characters are the trickiest to describe are the ones with an intrinsic connection to a specific branch of the setting's cosmology; &quot;the game about investigating all sorts of magical weirdness and Fortean phenomena privileges the perspective of magical researchers powered by the transcendent force that they use to describe reality and the evidence of their magical eyes over the teachings of their nonmagical upbringing that they broke from to become a wizard&quot; doesn't rate.</div> </div> </div> </div>Awakening is as Gnostic as Kult is. The broad outline is there, but the specifics of the cosmology is not. You do not have Sophia messing up the process of manifestation and you do not have Yaldabaoth keeping fragments of the Pleroma in a stupor. Awakening does not adhere to a Gnostic cosmology, and frankly, that is fine. To actually claim that it is Gnostic, however, is incorrect. It would be like claiming that Rain World is Buddhist. <br /> <br /> In regards to your latter points, the setting of Chronicles of Darkness as thaumaturges, as displayed in Second Sight. Mages and occultists co-exist in the same world. Thus, the primacy of the Supernal cosmology of Awakening necessarily has repercussions for real-world religions.<br /> <br /> Finally, not all mages would necessarily have nonmagical upbringings. There is no reason why individuals with a magical background could not Awaken.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28352-verlan","name":"Verlan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"118"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-14T06:03:23-07:00","id":1534187,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verlan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1532728-soul-mechanics-and-religious-mages?p=1534178#post1534178\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Awakening is as Gnostic as Kult is. The broad outline is there, but the specifics of the cosmology is not. You do not have Sophia messing up the process of manifestation and you do not have Yaldabaoth keeping fragments of the Pleroma in a stupor. Awakening does not adhere to a Gnostic cosmology, and frankly, that is fine. To actually claim that it is Gnostic, however, is incorrect. It would be like claiming that Rain World is Buddhist.</div> </div> </div> </div>Gnosticism isn't a singular religion of belief system. It's a collection of several different religious traditions that share specific themes, with wildly varying actual cosmologies. What you have described is <i>one</i> gnostic system.<br /> Not to mention that we do have the Fall (Sophia introducing imperfection to the world), and the Exarchs (Yaldabaoth/Demiurge) who are working to suppress the Oracles (Pleroma) who in turn guide humanity (through the Watchtowers Awakening people) in the mainline Diamond belief system.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer for <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/410713/Bloodlines-The-Ageless\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Ageless</a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/491569/Fire--Revolution-Carthians\" target=\"_blank\">Fire &amp; Revolution: Carthians</a> on STV<br /> Some other stuff I've done: Ordo Dracul Mysteries: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1138490-idea-coil-of-owls?p=1140522\" target=\"_blank\">Mystery of Smoke</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1227848-mystery-of-%C5%BEiva-house-rule-revision\" target=\"_blank\">Revised Mystery of \u017diva</a> Mage The Awakening: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B2LMDRtfCcpdZDU1dWJzT1dNeEE/view\" target=\"_blank\">Spell Quick Reference</a> (single page and landscape for computer screens)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","name":"Tessie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11887_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-14T07:29:59-07:00","id":1534193,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verlan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1532728-soul-mechanics-and-religious-mages?p=1534178#post1534178\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Awakening is as Gnostic as Kult is. The broad outline is there, but the specifics of the cosmology is not. You do not have Sophia messing up the process of manifestation and you do not have Yaldabaoth keeping fragments of the Pleroma in a stupor. Awakening does not adhere to a Gnostic cosmology, and frankly, that is fine. To actually claim that it is Gnostic, however, is incorrect. It would be like claiming that Rain World is Buddhist.</div> </div> </div> </div>Adding to Tessie's point, I don't think <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gnosticism_in_modern_times\" target=\"_blank\">Blavatsky, Jung, the Neo-Platonists and the like</a> need to qualify as more than a broad outline for Awakening to be Gnostic in the sense that most people who are not speaking in the role of religious scholars mean, which is in the same sense as the Matrix is a Gnostic setting.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In regards to your latter points, the setting of Chronicles of Darkness as thaumaturges, as displayed in Second Sight. Mages and occultists co-exist in the same world. Thus, the primacy of the Supernal cosmology of Awakening necessarily has repercussions for real-world religions. </div> </div> </div>Mages dealing with the Supernal has as much repercussions for real-world religions and occult practices as people with cash interacting with a world where most of the population uses vouchers and the barter system; magic systems that aren't Awakened use of the Arcana leverage established connections in a way that mages use as mnemonic aids. Without the transgressive steps off the beaten path necessary to pursue the Mysteries, practitioners of mortal magics interact with Supernal cosmology from roughly the same perspective as anyone else with one or more feet in the shadows \u2014 they're using an existing system directly where mages are building all their spells from scratch in a volatile manner (or developing their own system at a slower pace through Legacy Attainments).<br /> <br /> The hubris of the Awakened is most consistently portrayed as the belief that satisfying their curiosity is an end worth any means (and incurious people do not Awaken in the first place); if a character's beliefs about souls are never tested they can go on thinking what they like about it, but the Sects constitute a society with taboos specifically relating to souls as the setting describes them, and the breadth of possible magical acts are such that if you've been taught by the most knowledgeable group of mages in the setting and want to look into topics relating to the magical beliefs of the culture you were raised in then you have the tools to do so whether those beliefs survive the investigation or not.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Finally, not all mages would necessarily have nonmagical upbringings. There is no reason why individuals with a magical background could not Awaken. </div> </div> </div>We have explicit discussion of the reasons why knowing about the supernatural generally makes a character less likely to Awaken, but yes, this is technically true.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-14T11:46:14-07:00","id":1534204,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tessie</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1532728-soul-mechanics-and-religious-mages?p=1534187#post1534187\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Gnosticism isn't a singular religion of belief system. It's a collection of several different religious traditions that share specific themes, with wildly varying actual cosmologies. What you have described is <i>one</i> gnostic system.<br /> Not to mention that we do have the Fall (Sophia introducing imperfection to the world), and the Exarchs (Yaldabaoth/Demiurge) who are working to suppress the Oracles (Pleroma) who in turn guide humanity (through the Watchtowers Awakening people) in the mainline Diamond belief system.</div> </div> </div> </div>I know that there are many Gnostic systems. I only used that given one as an example. I used that example to illustrate how the cosmology of Awakening is not Gnostic apart from its epistemology. This is nothing new. In fact, most strains of western ceremonial magic have been Gnostic in regards to epistemology, but not cosmology. Essentially, one has to clarify which meaning of the term Gnostic that one is referring to. The term Gnostic is generally used to refer to religious systems that are predicated upon the importance of religious experience to the attainment of liberation, and Awakening does match that definition of the term. As I have pointed out, under that definition, certain religions could be defined as Gnostic, such as Jainism, even though their cosmology does not match the traditional Gnostic model. <br /> <br /> As an aside, I had not realized that you were a writer for Bloodlines: The Ageless. This is not related to the discussion at hand, but that supplement was quite good. Thank you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28352-verlan","name":"Verlan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"118"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-14T12:00:29-07:00","id":1534206,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1532728-soul-mechanics-and-religious-mages?p=1534193#post1534193\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Adding to Tessie's point, I don't think <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gnosticism_in_modern_times\" target=\"_blank\">Blavatsky, Jung, the Neo-Platonists and the like</a> need to qualify as more than a broad outline for Awakening to be Gnostic in the sense that most people who are not speaking in the role of religious scholars mean, which is in the same sense as the Matrix is a Gnostic setting.<br /> <br /> Mages dealing with the Supernal has as much repercussions for real-world religions and occult practices as people with cash interacting with a world where most of the population uses vouchers and the barter system; magic systems that aren't Awakened use of the Arcana leverage established connections in a way that mages use as mnemonic aids. Without the transgressive steps off the beaten path necessary to pursue the Mysteries, practitioners of mortal magics interact with Supernal cosmology from roughly the same perspective as anyone else with one or more feet in the shadows \u2014 they're using an existing system directly where mages are building all their spells from scratch in a volatile manner (or developing their own system at a slower pace through Legacy Attainments).<br /> <br /> The hubris of the Awakened is most consistently portrayed as the belief that satisfying their curiosity is an end worth any means (and incurious people do not Awaken in the first place); if a character's beliefs about souls are never tested they can go on thinking what they like about it, but the Sects constitute a society with taboos specifically relating to souls as the setting describes them, and the breadth of possible magical acts are such that if you've been taught by the most knowledgeable group of mages in the setting and want to look into topics relating to the magical beliefs of the culture you were raised in then you have the tools to do so whether those beliefs survive the investigation or not.<br /> <br /> We have explicit discussion of the reasons why knowing about the supernatural generally makes a character less likely to Awaken, but yes, this is technically true.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I think that bringing up Blavatsky and the like is actually quite pertinent. Theosophy and Neo-Platonism are topics that are distinct from Gnosticism per se. There are a great many similarities, of course, particularly in Theosophy, but the environments that they developed in were fundamentally different. Theosophy owes more to the occult lodges of the late 19th century and early 20th century than it does to Gnostic philosophy. Furthermore, it was influenced by Orientalism. Neo-Platonism did have some Gnostic influences given that it was post-Greek, but again, it was its own philosophy. I think the main problem is that books tend to generalize many religious and occult traditions into a single undifferentiated mass, when in reality they each had their own philosophy and identity. I think that some generalization is fine, but the matter becomes more troublesome when the Awakened are depicted as fundamentally more knowledgeable regarding the universe than the cosmologies that the writers based them upon.<br /> <br /> As for the matter of mortal occult practitioners, the problem is that they are depicted as not walking off of the beaten path, as is the case with the Awakened. Religious trailblazers are nothing new. Mages themselves are based upon such individuals. By supposing that mortal occultists do not have access to the Mysteries in the same way that mages do, one is necessarily making a significant cosmological presumption. Furthermore, the idea of thaumaturges utilizing already-manifested systems does not match the history of occult traditions. Magical systems grow and develop, like many things in the world. It feels like the presentation that you are describing renders thaumaturges essentially reliant on Awakened mages for the development of their magical systems.<br /> <br /> Finally, in regards to your last point, I do recall that Sleepwalkers are described as less likely to awaken than Sleepers. However, I do not remember if that is established as something that is known to be true, or something that is theorized by the Awakened.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28352-verlan","name":"Verlan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"118"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-14T19:32:56-07:00","id":1534222,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verlan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1532728-soul-mechanics-and-religious-mages?p=1534206#post1534206\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As for the matter of mortal occult practitioners, the problem is that they are depicted as not walking off of the beaten path, as is the case with the Awakened. Religious trailblazers are nothing new. Mages themselves are based upon such individuals. By supposing that mortal occultists do not have access to the Mysteries in the same way that mages do, one is necessarily making a significant cosmological presumption. Furthermore, the idea of thaumaturges utilizing already-manifested systems does not match the history of occult traditions. Magical systems grow and develop, like many things in the world. It feels like the presentation that you are describing renders thaumaturges essentially reliant on Awakened mages for the development of their magical systems.<br /> <br /> Finally, in regards to your last point, I do recall that Sleepwalkers are described as less likely to awaken than Sleepers. However, I do not remember if that is established as something that is known to be true, or something that is theorized by the Awakened.</div> </div> </div> </div>Verlan, have you read Signs of Sorcery, the book that talks about how the Awakening, one of the best-understood processes of becoming a supernatural in this setting, works? Have you looked at the explicit varieties of experience that Awakenings entail that include contending with the search for impossible truth and a novel confrontation with the Lie? Do you seriously believe that I or the books are positing that magical traditions got all their ideas from mages just because mages do not strictly require a magical tradition to work miracles?<br /> <br /> Mortal occultists objectively have less access to the supernatural than monsters like mages do. This is demonstrated by the fact that monsters with an innate connection to other worlds with Irises tend to at least have simple means of bypassing the Keys a mortal would otherwise need to determine for each individual gateway, to say nothing of magical senses like the ability to perceive specific frequencies of Twilight or the instinctive draw toward other monsters of their type. Mortal magicians are operating in the same sphere of development as krewes in Geist and sorcerer cults in Mummy, the former of which involves building a mythology for a death-religion whose teachings include magically efficacious ceremonies and the latter of which involves dangerous experimentation or tracking down hidden records of processes that work.<br /> <br /> A mage is a feedback loop of occult strangeness and the examination thereof. Symbolic support can help them bootstrap their workings higher and stretch the limits of their capabilities, but they can command the dead and twist destiny with nothing more than an exercise of will and a little luck where an unAwakened soul would need to gather reagents and seek out locations resonant with their goal. The two may theoretically both tap into the same force (in the sense that both use &quot;magic&quot; or &quot;the divine&quot; to do the things they do, but the former does not invalidate the latter's spiritual beliefs any more than vampires coming up with the Longinian Creed for their own purposes invalidates the Catholic doctrine of transubstantiation. The world exists and progresses with or without the Awakened putting their thumb on the scales.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-15T11:57:59-07:00","id":1534261,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1532728-soul-mechanics-and-religious-mages?p=1534222#post1534222\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Verlan, have you read Signs of Sorcery, the book that talks about how the Awakening, one of the best-understood processes of becoming a supernatural in this setting, works? Have you looked at the explicit varieties of experience that Awakenings entail that include contending with the search for impossible truth and a novel confrontation with the Lie? Do you seriously believe that I or the books are positing that magical traditions got all their ideas from mages just because mages do not strictly require a magical tradition to work miracles?<br /> <br /> Mortal occultists objectively have less access to the supernatural than monsters like mages do. This is demonstrated by the fact that monsters with an innate connection to other worlds with Irises tend to at least have simple means of bypassing the Keys a mortal would otherwise need to determine for each individual gateway, to say nothing of magical senses like the ability to perceive specific frequencies of Twilight or the instinctive draw toward other monsters of their type. Mortal magicians are operating in the same sphere of development as krewes in Geist and sorcerer cults in Mummy, the former of which involves building a mythology for a death-religion whose teachings include magically efficacious ceremonies and the latter of which involves dangerous experimentation or tracking down hidden records of processes that work.<br /> <br /> A mage is a feedback loop of occult strangeness and the examination thereof. Symbolic support can help them bootstrap their workings higher and stretch the limits of their capabilities, but they can command the dead and twist destiny with nothing more than an exercise of will and a little luck where an unAwakened soul would need to gather reagents and seek out locations resonant with their goal. The two may theoretically both tap into the same force (in the sense that both use &quot;magic&quot; or &quot;the divine&quot; to do the things they do, but the former does not invalidate the latter's spiritual beliefs any more than vampires coming up with the Longinian Creed for their own purposes invalidates the Catholic doctrine of transubstantiation. The world exists and progresses with or without the Awakened putting their thumb on the scales.</div> </div> </div> </div>I have read Signs of Sorcery and its description of the Awakening. To be honest, I feel like too much of the nature of Awakenings is detailed. On one hand, this can be seen as a positive, as it clearly differentiates Awakenings from other types of mystical experiences, and thus does not step on the toes of other religions that much. On the other hand, I feel like Awakenings are still presented in a way that suggests that they are more ontologically correct than the mystical experiences of mortals. I am fine with mages being substantially more powerful than mortal magicians; they are full supernaturals, after all. I am just concerned about mages being portrayed as possessing more correct cosmologies than mortals, particularly when such cosmologies are often a stew of various religious elements mashed together without the context of the original magical systems. As an aside, I also feel like some parts of Signs of Sorcery are not really understandable. For example, Signs of Sorcery says that prepubescent children cannot Awaken, but the attainment of adolescence seems like a pretty steep requirement. If anything, having lived for at least seven years sounds like a more reasonable requirement, given how the faculty of reason is often decently developed by that age.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28352-verlan","name":"Verlan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"118"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-15T14:51:56-07:00","id":1534268,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verlan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1532728-soul-mechanics-and-religious-mages?p=1534261#post1534261\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">On the other hand, I feel like Awakenings are still presented in a way that suggests that they are more ontologically correct than the mystical experiences of mortals.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is a nonsensical statement without defining what you mean by &quot;the mystical experiences of mortals&quot; and &quot;more ontologically correct.&quot; In a setting where the supernatural is pervasive and mages in particular know it's nigh-on inescapable, a mystical experience could be a psychological breakthrough, a bout of mental illness, an ordinary <i>eureka</i> moment in the right context, deliberate or inadvertent communion with local or extradimensional supernatural entities, or any number of stranger things, and the only distinction the Awakening has over any of those is that it interfaces with symbolism that taps into the Truth and potentially ends in a character becoming a mage.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I am just concerned about mages being portrayed as possessing more correct cosmologies than mortals, particularly when such cosmologies are often a stew of various religious elements mashed together without the context of the original magical systems. </div> </div> </div>A game set in a cosmology where there are several consistent major supernatural forces that do not change with human culture on the timescale of a mortal lifespan and an organized group of wizards has been studying the supernatural as a whole for thousands of years cannot reasonably lend gameline-level weight to the cosmology of every cultural perspective on the world and its magic.<br /> <br /> This is a setting where &quot;mages have met the Astral manifestations of the gods they worshiped and either become disillusioned with their faith or pinned their hopes on a True version of those deities in the Supernal&quot; has been a thing for some time, to say nothing of Temenos realms making magic that conforms to their conventions easier or Supernal Verges and Emanation Realms having odd physical laws and magical objects within their boundaries; magical traditions can have supernatural truth to them without needing to have a direct line to Real Ultimate Power\u2122.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As an aside, I also feel like some parts of Signs of Sorcery are not really understandable. For example, Signs of Sorcery says that prepubescent children cannot Awaken, but the attainment of adolescence seems like a pretty steep requirement. If anything, having lived for at least seven years sounds like a more reasonable requirement, given how the faculty of reason is often decently developed by that age. </div> </div> </div>I am legitimately confused as to how &quot;the faculty of reason&quot; can be equated with the ability to confront the Lie while one if still learning the Lie. It would be like expecting to overturn everything you knew about physics and revolutionizing the study of energy before you even learned what gravity is \u2014 there's no &quot;everything I thought I knew is a Lie&quot; moment when you don't know shit and you know it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-15T19:58:31-07:00","id":1534279,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1532728-soul-mechanics-and-religious-mages?p=1534268#post1534268\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is a nonsensical statement without defining what you mean by &quot;the mystical experiences of mortals&quot; and &quot;more ontologically correct.&quot; In a setting where the supernatural is pervasive and mages in particular know it's nigh-on inescapable, a mystical experience could be a psychological breakthrough, a bout of mental illness, an ordinary <i>eureka</i> moment in the right context, deliberate or inadvertent communion with local or extradimensional supernatural entities, or any number of stranger things, and the only distinction the Awakening has over any of those is that it interfaces with symbolism that taps into the Truth and potentially ends in a character becoming a mage.<br /> <br /> A game set in a cosmology where there are several consistent major supernatural forces that do not change with human culture on the timescale of a mortal lifespan and an organized group of wizards has been studying the supernatural as a whole for thousands of years cannot reasonably lend gameline-level weight to the cosmology of every cultural perspective on the world and its magic.<br /> <br /> This is a setting where &quot;mages have met the Astral manifestations of the gods they worshiped and either become disillusioned with their faith or pinned their hopes on a True version of those deities in the Supernal&quot; has been a thing for some time, to say nothing of Temenos realms making magic that conforms to their conventions easier or Supernal Verges and Emanation Realms having odd physical laws and magical objects within their boundaries; magical traditions can have supernatural truth to them without needing to have a direct line to Real Ultimate Power\u2122.<br /> <br /> I am legitimately confused as to how &quot;the faculty of reason&quot; can be equated with the ability to confront the Lie while one if still learning the Lie. It would be like expecting to overturn everything you knew about physics and revolutionizing the study of energy before you even learned what gravity is \u2014 there's no &quot;everything I thought I knew is a Lie&quot; moment when you don't know shit and you know it.</div> </div> </div> </div>In regards to your first point, I was referring to religious experiences. In particular, I meant religious experiences in general, and not just in the context of the game. It is pretty clear that the Awakening is inspired by the religious experience. This is something that is explicitly mentioned in regards to the Obrimos Path.<br /> <br /> Also, I think that you raise an important point when you mention the Supernal. Not all cosmologies would posit that the Supernal is the highest form of existence.<br /> <br /> In regards to the last point, I think that you overestimate the amount of information that one must acquire before they can realize that reality is a sham. In particular, I do not think that realizing that certain physical laws are wrong would be necessary to realize that on a fundamental level, what one calls reality is in fact illusory and meaningless.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28352-verlan","name":"Verlan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"118"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}